1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,860
Well, good morning, Canvas.

2
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:10,960
Thanks so much for joining us this morning, whether you're here at the Alamo, you're watching

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:11,320
online.

4
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:12,100
My name is Ryan.

5
00:00:12,100 --> 00:00:15,860
I had the privilege of being one of the pastors here at Canvas.

6
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:20,380
I walked in this morning, and I was getting a hard time from some of our people for not

7
00:00:20,380 --> 00:00:21,320
dressing up for Halloween.

8
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:29,600
And I told them I dressed up like a real pastor, because if you've been around Canvas for long

9
00:00:29,600 --> 00:00:33,560
at all, you know that our lead pastor is covered in tattoos, and our associate pastor usually

10
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:34,680
wears shorts and a t-shirt.

11
00:00:34,680 --> 00:00:37,760
So I put dress pants and a dress shirt on.

12
00:00:37,760 --> 00:00:39,380
This is my Halloween costume.

13
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:41,160
I am a real pastor this morning.

14
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:48,360
So excited to be here to wrap up our series through the book of Daniel.

15
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:53,700
Today is going to be a little bit different than most of the sermons that we have at Canvas.

16
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:59,120
We are actually kind of summarizing the whole last half of the book of Daniel in one message,

17
00:00:59,200 --> 00:01:01,540
because it's on the end times and prophecy.

18
00:01:01,540 --> 00:01:03,680
And it worked out perfectly, because again, it's Halloween.

19
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:07,540
We're going to read about some beasts that have horns coming out of their heads.

20
00:01:07,540 --> 00:01:10,760
One of the horns grows eyes and starts talking like a human.

21
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:14,460
It really is a perfect passage for Halloween.

22
00:01:14,460 --> 00:01:21,200
But I'm excited and also a little bit anxious, because any time you start talking about end times

23
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,940
and eschatology, some people want to leave because they don't want to talk about it at all.

24
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:31,740
And other people are obsessed with it and have maps that they've hand-drawn of exactly what they think is going to happen

25
00:01:31,740 --> 00:01:32,600
and how it's going to happen.

26
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:37,180
And we'll talk a little bit about both of those people more as we go on this morning.

27
00:01:37,680 --> 00:01:43,260
But the message really is going to be a little bit different, because I really have two messages built into one.

28
00:01:43,260 --> 00:01:47,400
One, the overarching message is I want to talk to us about how we should read prophecy.

29
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:53,260
Like, I want to give us an overview so that when we find ourselves reading prophetic passages on our own,

30
00:01:53,600 --> 00:01:57,540
we kind of have an overview, an idea of how to read those things.

31
00:01:57,540 --> 00:01:59,360
There's an application that's going to come with that.

32
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:05,300
And then inside of that overarching message, we're just going to walk through Daniel 7, which is a prophetic passage.

33
00:02:05,300 --> 00:02:11,100
And I'm just going to show you as an example of how we would read that using that overarching message.

34
00:02:11,100 --> 00:02:11,760
Does that make sense?

35
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:13,100
Hopefully.

36
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:13,460
All right.

37
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,640
That's kind of how we're going to go with it.

38
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:20,000
Just to review where we've been so that you know where we're at so far,

39
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,260
we've said throughout this series in Daniel that the Bible isn't just about what happens,

40
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:26,800
but it's about what always happens.

41
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:29,580
And what we mean by that is Daniel's a real character.

42
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:30,840
Babylon's a real place.

43
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:35,620
We believe these things happened, and they were recorded for us for a reason.

44
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:39,280
God preserved the story of Daniel for us for a reason.

45
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,680
He preserved the narrative section of Daniel to encourage our hearts,

46
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:47,260
to show us that believers who find themselves in secular nations,

47
00:02:47,260 --> 00:02:50,320
nations that don't want to follow the things of God,

48
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:53,860
that we as believers, we can stay true and faithful to the things of God,

49
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:57,280
and we can have an impact in our world, in our culture,

50
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:00,840
even when our culture disagrees with our values.

51
00:03:00,840 --> 00:03:05,360
So the book of Daniel was written to inspire hope in the hearts of believers in every generation.

52
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:10,160
But today, as we turn our eyes to the prophetic section of Daniel,

53
00:03:11,080 --> 00:03:16,620
we believe that all prophecy in the Bible was written to give hope to hurting people.

54
00:03:16,620 --> 00:03:19,400
Like whenever we see prophecy in Scripture,

55
00:03:19,400 --> 00:03:22,060
it's always at a time when God's people are struggling,

56
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:23,860
when they're in bondage, when they're in slavery.

57
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:29,100
And God gives these prophetic visions of the future to remind His people

58
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:31,980
that He is sovereign and in control of all things.

59
00:03:31,980 --> 00:03:36,060
And even if they can't see Him working out all things together for good,

60
00:03:36,240 --> 00:03:39,080
they can hold fast and believe it in the midst of struggle,

61
00:03:39,080 --> 00:03:42,700
they can know that ultimately one day God is going to come back

62
00:03:42,700 --> 00:03:43,940
and He's going to right every wrong,

63
00:03:43,940 --> 00:03:47,200
and He's going to bring peace and justice to the land.

64
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:49,620
And so when we look at prophetic Scriptures,

65
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:52,920
we should be getting hope, we should be inspired for the future,

66
00:03:52,920 --> 00:03:55,060
even if we can't see it in the present.

67
00:03:55,060 --> 00:04:00,080
A couple of disclaimers up front before we get into the specifics.

68
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:04,140
Prophecy often brings with it a discussion about eschatology.

69
00:04:04,140 --> 00:04:08,480
Eschatology is a fancy word that theologians use to make themselves feel smart.

70
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:10,340
It just means end times.

71
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:16,700
And so everybody, there's multiple views on the end times,

72
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:20,220
and oftentimes some people are very passionate about their views of the end times.

73
00:04:20,220 --> 00:04:24,500
And some people have no clue if they even have a view of the end times, right?

74
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,880
And so there's people all over the spectrum.

75
00:04:26,880 --> 00:04:33,320
And I recently heard a pastor say that every view of eschatology comes with its assets and its liabilities.

76
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:36,160
Like there's no perfect framework.

77
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:39,820
There's no perfect structure that makes everything make sense perfectly.

78
00:04:39,820 --> 00:04:41,520
And so we should hold those things loosely.

79
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:43,920
We should hold those things with an open hand.

80
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:48,480
Some of you in here, you might have a firm grasp on what you believe about the end times,

81
00:04:48,480 --> 00:04:50,680
about how it's all going to work out.

82
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:56,340
Some of you might have no clue and maybe are kind of dreading the fact that you're even here this morning.

83
00:04:56,340 --> 00:04:58,200
You're like, man, I came to the end times message.

84
00:04:58,200 --> 00:04:58,520
Darn.

85
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:01,240
I find myself somewhere in the middle.

86
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:05,840
And so no matter what level of certainty or passion you have when it comes to eschatology,

87
00:05:05,840 --> 00:05:09,940
let's make sure that our hope is firmly planted in the right place.

88
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:14,700
Let's be a people who are certain and passionate that Jesus will return.

89
00:05:15,040 --> 00:05:19,000
And we hold up all of our other opinions in the open hand.

90
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,340
So that's kind of my disclaimer, where we're headed this morning.

91
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:25,960
Let me pray, and then we'll get into the specifics in Daniel chapter 7.

92
00:05:25,960 --> 00:05:32,760
Father, we need you to be present and to be in this room teaching us your word.

93
00:05:32,760 --> 00:05:37,360
Holy Spirit, I pray that each individual in this room would hear something from you this morning

94
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:43,200
that encourages them, that challenges them, that points them to the message of hope that we have in Jesus.

95
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:49,600
I pray that we would leave this place hearing from you and knowing what you would have for us,

96
00:05:49,600 --> 00:05:51,620
knowing that we have a hope for the future.

97
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:53,740
God, conform us into your image.

98
00:05:53,740 --> 00:05:55,960
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

99
00:05:55,960 --> 00:05:56,980
Amen.

100
00:05:56,980 --> 00:06:01,300
If you have your Bibles, you want to turn to Daniel chapter 7, you can do so.

101
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:06,860
We'll get there in a little bit as I've been – this might be the longest intro that I ever do.

102
00:06:07,020 --> 00:06:11,880
So if we get, like, halfway through the service and then we get into Daniel 7, I'm sorry, but we'll get there eventually.

103
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:14,960
So turn in your Bibles to Daniel chapter 7.

104
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:21,840
As you're turning, I'd like to ask you, how many of you in this room have known what this is or have ever used one of these?

105
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:23,300
We've got some hands.

106
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:24,780
This is a rangefinder.

107
00:06:25,500 --> 00:06:31,140
Some of you might use rangefinders in hunting or golfing or maybe birdwatching.

108
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:35,460
If you guys are real intense birdwatchers, maybe you've used rangefinders in the past.

109
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:37,940
But rangefinders are cool.

110
00:06:37,940 --> 00:06:45,360
It's kind of like a single-lens binocular, and you can look downrange, and you can see a very small field of view very clearly,

111
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:47,320
and everything else is kind of difficult to see.

112
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:51,520
And you can lock on to things, and it'll tell you how far certain things are away.

113
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:58,840
And most of my exposure – I have tried hunting recently, so this is actually a hunting rangefinder that I stole from a friend.

114
00:06:58,840 --> 00:07:02,900
But most of my experience with rangefinders come on the golf course.

115
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:05,540
And I remember this time I was playing golf with a good friend of mine.

116
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:08,040
His name is Luke, and he's very good at golf.

117
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:10,180
I am very much not.

118
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:19,900
But what happens when you play golf with somebody who's very good at golf is they know just about exactly how far each of their clubs is going to hit the ball.

119
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:26,900
And so if they have a shot that's 150 yards, you pull the rangefinder out, shoot it, they know what club goes 150 yards.

120
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:29,120
They can adjust for the wind and the slope.

121
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:36,360
They do all these calculations in their head, and they can be pretty confident that once they hit the ball, it's going to go the distance that they want it to go.

122
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:40,780
And I was golfing with Luke one time, and he tossed the rangefinder to his buddy, Stephen.

123
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:43,580
He said, how far is the flag, or how far is the hole?

124
00:07:43,580 --> 00:07:47,580
And Stephen shot down range and said, it's 160 yards.

125
00:07:47,580 --> 00:07:51,740
So Luke said, okay, he knows how far his club that goes 160 yards goes.

126
00:07:51,740 --> 00:07:52,540
He hits the ball.

127
00:07:52,540 --> 00:07:55,440
It's flying right at the flag, perfect trajectory.

128
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:59,960
And all of us that aren't good at golf are watching the ball like, man, I wish my ball looked like that.

129
00:07:59,960 --> 00:08:00,520
It never does.

130
00:08:00,520 --> 00:08:04,400
And it landed in the sand trap behind the hole.

131
00:08:04,980 --> 00:08:12,860
And Luke looked down at the ground, and he looked at his club, and he looked at his friend, Stephen, and he was like, did you say it was 160 yards?

132
00:08:12,860 --> 00:08:14,120
And he's like, yeah, it's 160 yards.

133
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,320
Give me the rangefinder, because he knows.

134
00:08:16,320 --> 00:08:17,240
He's a good golfer, right?

135
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:17,540
He knows.

136
00:08:17,540 --> 00:08:19,500
I just hit that ball 160 yards.

137
00:08:19,500 --> 00:08:24,500
And so he looks down the thing, and he said, you gave me the distance to the sand trap.

138
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:26,040
The hole's 150 yards.

139
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:27,500
The sand trap's 160 yards.

140
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:28,720
I hit it 160 yards.

141
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:31,660
And that's why I went into the sand trap.

142
00:08:31,660 --> 00:08:37,840
And I think this perfectly illustrates for us our main point this morning about prophecy.

143
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:51,180
Because I think prophecy, as we look at Daniel chapter 7 and other prophetic passages of Scripture, oftentimes the writers of those prophecies, they're looking down rings through this small lens.

144
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:52,960
And they can see some things clearly.

145
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:55,460
They're getting imagery and pictures from God.

146
00:08:55,460 --> 00:09:00,540
But they don't exactly always know when it's going to happen or how it's all going to unfold.

147
00:09:00,680 --> 00:09:07,700
Sometimes they think they might be locking in on something, and they know, like, oh, this must be about to happen soon, and it's going to be in the future.

148
00:09:07,700 --> 00:09:18,980
And so I think a rangefinder, as we look into this passage, is going to show us how we should read prophecy and ultimately illustrates for us my main point for this morning, and that is this.

149
00:09:18,980 --> 00:09:28,480
We're going to keep coming back to this over and over and over again, that all prophecy points us to the first coming of Jesus or prepares us for his second coming.

150
00:09:28,580 --> 00:09:34,900
When we look at prophecy in Scripture, all of it is either about the first coming of Jesus or the second coming of Jesus.

151
00:09:34,900 --> 00:09:39,000
The whole point of prophecy is to point us to Jesus.

152
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:43,200
And we find hope and encouragement in that.

153
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:49,380
And so what we should do when we look at Scripture, Davis, you can throw that diagram up for me real quick.

154
00:09:49,380 --> 00:09:56,500
What we should do when we look at prophecy is we should put ourselves in the position of the author, right?

155
00:09:56,560 --> 00:10:01,320
And most authors of prophecy are on this side of the first coming of Jesus.

156
00:10:01,320 --> 00:10:02,340
We've got creation.

157
00:10:02,340 --> 00:10:07,980
We've got God's people in secular culture are all coming into the first coming of Jesus.

158
00:10:07,980 --> 00:10:12,000
And Daniel is going to be somewhere over here on the left of this red X.

159
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:15,280
And what he's doing is he's looking down range.

160
00:10:15,280 --> 00:10:21,000
And he's seeing visions and prophecies about the first coming of Jesus and about the second coming of Jesus.

161
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:25,720
And he doesn't really know exactly what he's seeing or what it all means.

162
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:28,220
But we have the privilege of hindsight, right?

163
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:32,880
Like we get to look back and try and work out some of this stuff when it comes to history.

164
00:10:32,880 --> 00:10:36,480
So that's kind of what we're doing and how we should look at prophecy.

165
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:40,960
First Peter, chapter 1, says this about the prophets.

166
00:10:40,960 --> 00:10:50,000
It says this salvation was something that the prophets wanted to know more about when they prophesied about the gracious salvation prepared for you.

167
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:56,100
So guys like Daniel were writing prophecy about Jesus, and they're asking God, like, what is this all about?

168
00:10:56,100 --> 00:10:58,080
How is this all going to play out?

169
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:08,040
They wondered what time the salvation that the Spirit of Christ within them was talking about when he told them in advance about Christ's suffering and his great glory afterward.

170
00:11:08,040 --> 00:11:12,000
They were told that their message was not for themselves but for you.

171
00:11:12,220 --> 00:11:20,320
And now, this good news has been announced to you by those who preached in the power of the Holy Spirit who were sent from heaven.

172
00:11:20,320 --> 00:11:27,620
It is all so wonderful that even the angels eagerly watched these things happen.

173
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:29,320
So we think about prophecy.

174
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:34,160
Prophecy either points us to the first coming of Jesus or prepares us for his second coming.

175
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:37,520
And God's working all of this out in some incredible ways.

176
00:11:37,520 --> 00:11:51,440
So incredible and so amazing are the ways that God is working these things out, that the angels are in heaven watching it unfold, watching history unfold and unravel in such a way that the first coming of Jesus comes at the perfect time in the perfect way.

177
00:11:51,440 --> 00:11:53,180
And they're like, wow, I never would have seen that.

178
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:54,980
I never would have designed something like that.

179
00:11:54,980 --> 00:11:57,340
But our God is so good and so perfect.

180
00:11:57,480 --> 00:12:10,060
And so they see the first coming of Jesus and now they're in heaven just anticipating how God's going to continue to unfold in human history to bring about the second coming of Jesus.

181
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:26,080
And so when we get into prophecy, I know it might be scary or difficult or seem overwhelming, but at the end of the day, all of it should give us hope because all of it is supposed to point us to the first coming or prepare us for the second coming.

182
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:27,200
Are we tracking so far?

183
00:12:27,480 --> 00:12:33,080
All right, so that's kind of my overarching message that we're going to draw application from throughout the day.

184
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:42,380
But now that we have that framework, let's hop into Daniel chapter 7 and use that framework as we just use Daniel chapter 7 as an example.

185
00:12:42,380 --> 00:12:47,820
And what I'm going to do here is walk through the traditional view of Daniel chapter 7.

186
00:12:47,820 --> 00:12:54,520
There's other views of Daniel chapter 7, but I think the traditional view is best, and I think it works within this framework.

187
00:12:54,520 --> 00:12:56,980
Because what we're going to see in Daniel chapter 7 is two things.

188
00:12:57,980 --> 00:13:02,440
We're going to see a vision about four beasts with horns and all kinds of stuff.

189
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:03,220
It's going to be fun.

190
00:13:03,220 --> 00:13:08,800
And I believe that those beasts are going to ultimately point us to the first coming of Jesus.

191
00:13:08,800 --> 00:13:15,460
And then we're going to see a vision about the Antichrist and the Son of Man, and I believe that those things are about the second coming of Jesus, right?

192
00:13:15,580 --> 00:13:21,580
So it's like this framework that all of these things are about Jesus, but sometimes we're looking downrange, right?

193
00:13:21,580 --> 00:13:28,520
Like we're looking through this rangefinder, and it's not clear what things are going to come first and what things are going to come second and how it's going to all work out.

194
00:13:28,580 --> 00:13:41,620
So my first overarching application for us as we think about prophecies, we think about the end times, and all of it pointing to Jesus is this,

195
00:13:41,620 --> 00:13:48,160
that overconfidence in the specifics of our eschatology can easily knock us off in the end.

196
00:13:49,100 --> 00:13:54,980
Like if you're one of those people that are fixated on the end times, that are fixated on the book of Revelation and prophecy,

197
00:13:54,980 --> 00:14:01,360
and you read it all the time, and you've got those hand-drawn maps, and you think you know exactly when it's going to happen in exactly what order,

198
00:14:01,360 --> 00:14:05,280
and you're trying to figure out which bowl of wrath is being opened up right now,

199
00:14:05,280 --> 00:14:16,760
and if Vladimir Putin is the Antichrist and all of those things, I would just say that overconfidence in eschatology can easily knock us off our mission.

200
00:14:16,760 --> 00:14:26,860
I don't know that Canvas is full of people with hand-drawn maps, but I'm sure there's some of us that tend to put our overconfidence in eschatology.

201
00:14:26,860 --> 00:14:32,300
These people often have bunkers in their houses with, you know, enough canned food for like 10 years.

202
00:14:33,480 --> 00:14:37,960
But we see this happen around the first coming of Jesus, right?

203
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:45,180
The Israelites were so confident in their view of eschatology, they thought the Messiah was going to come as a powerful political leader.

204
00:14:45,180 --> 00:14:56,120
So when Jesus came as a suffering servant, they totally missed their promised Messiah because they had an overconfidence in their view of eschatology, right?

205
00:14:56,120 --> 00:15:03,360
So like Jesus comes, and he rides on the back of a donkey into Jerusalem, and there's a crowd of Israelites, and they say,

206
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:06,480
Hosanna, which means Messiah, save now.

207
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:07,340
So they're getting it.

208
00:15:07,340 --> 00:15:10,960
Like, they see that Jesus is this Messiah, this sent one of God.

209
00:15:10,960 --> 00:15:18,840
But then he gets arrested by the Romans, and they're like, this can't be the Messiah because the Messiah is supposed to come as a powerful political leader.

210
00:15:18,840 --> 00:15:24,800
